+
+## role
+The ARIA role this view presents to assistive technology (`"button"`, `"radio"`,
+`"dialog"`, `"status"`, …); `""` (the default) is a plain container. Views are painted, so
+nothing is implied: a view with `onClick` is **not** a button to a screen reader until it says
+so. A role makes the view able to hold real platform focus (programmatically — Tab order stays
+the focus service's), so focus a role-carrying view with `Focus.focus` and assistive technology
+follows. On a `TextInput` it lands on the native element. DOM-realized; a no-op on canvas.
 
+## aria
+ARIA states and properties as a record, keyed **without** the `aria-` prefix:
+`aria = { { label: "Orchestra, row C, seat 14", pressed: chosen, disabled: gone ? true : null } }`.
+Booleans render as `"true"`/`"false"` (so `pressed: false` is meaningful); `null`, `undefined`
+and `""` remove the attribute, and a key absent from the next record is removed. A constraint
+like any other — the announcement stays true as the data changes. `label` names a view whose
+text content would not (a drawn mark, a composed row); `live: "polite"` makes a status region;
+`hidden: true` takes a subtree out of the accessibility tree (the page behind a modal sheet).
+
 ## selectable
 Opt this run back into native text selection / copy (default `false`). Off by default
 so an app doesn't feel like a document; turn it on for content a user should be able to
